WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.020 --> 00:00:03.000
Did you know that title seven of the United States Code

00:00:03.000 --> 00:00:09.200
Section 8313(A)1(B) allows for a five-year sentence in the federal slammer for selling llama poop?

00:00:10.940 --> 00:00:15.520
Are you aware that Title 21 Part 139 of the Code of Federal Regulations

00:00:15.520 --> 00:00:18.540
prohibits the sale of improperly shaped spaghetti noodles?

00:00:20.100 --> 00:00:24.320
Or that the swine Health Protection Act forbids the feeding of garbage to a pig

00:00:24.320 --> 00:00:29.060
unless said garbage has been cooked by a garbage cooker who holds a garbage cooking permit?

00:00:31.540 --> 00:00:33.360
You didn't know any of these things

00:00:33.360 --> 00:00:38.080
but that's because you're not Mike Chase. Criminal defense attorney and author of the new book,

00:00:38.080 --> 00:00:42.960
"How to Become a Federal Criminal", an illustrated handbook for the aspiring offender.

00:00:42.960 --> 00:00:48.420
Perhaps the world's foremost expert on vague and dumb laws, the man behind the @CrimeADay twitter handle,

00:00:48.420 --> 00:00:51.420
has scoured through countless criminal statutes and codes

00:00:51.420 --> 00:00:56.160
in order to demonstrate the power of the federal government at its most absurd.

00:00:56.160 --> 00:01:00.940
I sat down with chase to learn the roots of his obsession with laws against improperly shaped cheese,

00:01:00.960 --> 00:01:05.380
whistling on a CB radio, and making unreasonable gestures to a passing horse.

00:01:08.220 --> 00:01:14.520
"How to Become a Federal Criminal" is sort of the      desk guide to all of the most

00:01:14.520 --> 00:01:18.720
arcane and ridiculous federal crimes that you could commit in the United States.

00:01:18.720 --> 00:01:23.060
And there are a combination of statutory violations and regulatory violations,

00:01:23.060 --> 00:01:26.440
but things like selling graded cream cheese,

00:01:26.440 --> 00:01:29.880
or you know walking on the grass at the Capitol.    Things like that.

00:01:29.880 --> 00:01:31.360
What are some of the more ridiculous ones?

00:01:31.360 --> 00:01:32.760
Can you give us more, some more examples?

00:01:32.760 --> 00:01:35.440
Some of them are really pretty hilarious.

00:01:35.440 --> 00:01:41.720
A personal favorite of mine is giving an unreasonable gesture to a passing horse in a national park.

00:01:43.180 --> 00:01:49.320
The law doesn't define what an unreasonable gesture is, and so you can kind of get creative I think.

00:01:50.260 --> 00:01:56.240
And I go through a few of my ideas for obscene gestures that you could make to a horse in the book

00:01:56.240 --> 00:01:59.880
But the regulator's don't give you sufficient guidance.

00:01:59.880 --> 00:02:04.240
And in a lot of cases all Congress does is basically say,

00:02:04.240 --> 00:02:09.080
hey look any violation of any regulation that the Secretary of the Interior makes,

00:02:09.080 --> 00:02:10.940
that's a federal crime.

00:02:10.940 --> 00:02:14.000
So oftentimes you have to go all the way to the Supreme Court

00:02:14.000 --> 00:02:16.380
to find out whether you've committed a crime or not.

00:02:16.380 --> 00:02:22.580
It is a pretty hilarious book. A lot of the illustrations and the examples are very very funny.

00:02:22.580 --> 00:02:26.360
But I'm curious, there's a serious undercurrent to this, right?

00:02:26.400 --> 00:02:28.040
Why does this all matter?

00:02:28.040 --> 00:02:32.540
Is it just shits and giggles or is there some kind of underlying importance to all this?

00:02:32.540 --> 00:02:36.420
There really is a serious underpinning. I mean people have been giving different names to it.

00:02:36.420 --> 00:02:40.840
We've talked about executive overreach, we've talked about over-regulation, over-criminalization.

00:02:40.840 --> 00:02:43.480
There's a whole bunch of names for it, but the truth is

00:02:43.480 --> 00:02:46.740
that when the country was founded there were basically three federal crimes

00:02:46.740 --> 00:02:48.220
and a government of limited powers.

00:02:48.220 --> 00:02:52.820
So it was treason, counterfeiting, and being a pirate basically were the crimes.

00:02:52.820 --> 00:02:58.280
And for a long time after that maybe the total crimes on the federal books were about 20

00:02:58.280 --> 00:03:02.900
But now some scholars have estimated that that number exceeds 300,000.

00:03:02.900 --> 00:03:04.760
And here's really what the problem is,

00:03:04.760 --> 00:03:11.440
The problem is if you can't know whether you're committing a crime or not, is it really a crime?

00:03:11.440 --> 00:03:17.820
And how much respect for the more morally offensive laws on the books will there be,

00:03:17.820 --> 00:03:20.160
if you can never really know if you're committing a crime?

00:03:20.160 --> 00:03:24.480
Yeah and for each law that you mention, as nutty as it is,

00:03:24.480 --> 00:03:27.860
you really do go into how we got into this mess to begin with.

00:03:27.860 --> 00:03:32.070
The funny thing is with all this law, with all this regulation and all the statute,

00:03:32.070 --> 00:03:37.580
it's very hard for a person to say what's in the books. And what's what's there.

00:03:37.580 --> 00:03:40.560
And so people sort of line up on different sides of the debate

00:03:40.560 --> 00:03:45.600
they either say regulation is inherently good or regulation is inherently bad.

00:03:45.600 --> 00:03:52.800
The answer is it's really a mix. Sometimes a law is really truly trying to protect people from some harm out there,

00:03:52.800 --> 00:03:59.040
but maybe it goes too far, maybe it misses the mark. Maybe it's been applied unconstitutionally

00:03:59.040 --> 00:04:03.560
and so it was important to delve into the reasons for these laws because some are well-intentioned,

00:04:03.560 --> 00:04:07.220
but they may be over-broad, too vague, or outdated.

00:04:07.220 --> 00:04:13.280
And as ridiculous as they are, nobody sets out to make a preposterous law and put it on the books.

00:04:13.280 --> 00:04:18.640
What is the source of a lot of these, there are certain categories that seem to provoke bizarre laws?

00:04:18.640 --> 00:04:21.080
We all know that the Constitution gives Congress

00:04:21.080 --> 00:04:25.200
certain powers, right? They can regulate commerce. They can regulate the high seas.

00:04:25.200 --> 00:04:32.380
you know they can, certain territories, right. Commerce being probably the most broad statutory authority.

00:04:32.380 --> 00:04:37.260
But they basically give blank checks to agencies, and say hey look,

00:04:37.260 --> 00:04:41.660
any violation of any regulation on a particular topic that you write,

00:04:41.660 --> 00:04:43.140
that's criminally enforceable.

00:04:43.140 --> 00:04:51.180
And so where the action is I'll say, is food, public land, on high seas,

00:04:51.180 --> 00:04:54.280
and money, for example, obviously is another place.

00:04:54.280 --> 00:04:57.340
And one of my personal favorite is in the mail, right,

00:04:57.340 --> 00:05:01.520
because everything that passes through the mail is potentially a federal crime.

00:05:01.520 --> 00:05:02.880
And poop.

00:05:02.880 --> 00:05:06.020
Poop is on the top of my list of things.

00:05:06.020 --> 00:05:09.580
Poop and animal semen are about the the best kind of crimes that you can commit

00:05:09.580 --> 00:05:15.060
because there's a shockingly high number of poop and semen crimes on the books.

00:05:15.060 --> 00:05:18.940
Congress does seem to have some sort of scatological fixation with llama poop, right?

00:05:18.940 --> 00:05:22.100
The shoveling llama poop in wrong places could be a federal offense?

00:05:22.100 --> 00:05:26.860
Any llama that comes to this country has a mandatory quarantine period when they get here.

00:05:26.860 --> 00:05:28.540
It's like thirty days or something like that.

00:05:28.540 --> 00:05:31.660
And you cannot go visit your llama without permission.

00:05:31.660 --> 00:05:36.220
And that can be a heartbreaking time in any llama owners life obviously.

00:05:36.220 --> 00:05:38.000
But if you want to bring home some poop—

00:05:38.000 --> 00:05:38.700
—Mm-hmm—

00:05:38.700 --> 00:05:43.580
—you know, just to remind you of your llama, that is a quarantine violation.

00:05:43.580 --> 00:05:47.760
Are these special interests drawing these laws? What is the sort of genesis of a lot of them?

00:05:47.760 --> 00:05:51.060
I will admit there's not a llama poop special interest, right?

00:05:51.060 --> 00:05:54.260
But yeah, the genesis of a lot of regulations are special interests are special interest.

00:05:54.260 --> 00:05:59.500
So here's a great example, one of the earliest kind of federal crimes that came about

00:05:59.500 --> 00:06:04.860
that was a violation of these regulations made by executive branch agencies was in margarine.

00:06:04.860 --> 00:06:10.260
And anybody can guess where the margarine regulation came from. It was big butter, right?

00:06:10.260 --> 00:06:15.500
And so the dairy industry came in and they said, we don't like this new kid on the block.

00:06:15.500 --> 00:06:21.700
It's every bit as tasty as our product and people seem to not know the difference in mind, and it's cheaper.

00:06:21.700 --> 00:06:24.700
And so the dairy lobby went to Washington and said,

00:06:24.700 --> 00:06:27.900
we need comprehensive butter control.

00:06:27.900 --> 00:06:34.500
And they got it. They got a butter bill passed. It was actually was a law called an act defining butter,

00:06:34.500 --> 00:06:39.140
And it required all kinds of crazy things and some of it remains on the books today.

00:06:39.140 --> 00:06:43.260
Interesting it's like the bacon law, right? The packaging for bacon. I found that one really interesting.

00:06:43.260 --> 00:06:49.240
It explained a longtime fascination as to why every package of bacon you can actually see the bacon in it.

00:06:49.240 --> 00:06:50.340
Yeah the bacon window.

00:06:50.340 --> 00:06:51.220
—The bacon window.—

00:06:51.240 --> 00:06:56.669
I call it the window of truth because I think that it really is the purpose that it serves and yeah, of course

00:06:56.669 --> 00:06:58.500
It's to tell the consumer,

00:06:58.500 --> 00:07:03.240
Hey, this is how fatty the bacon you are getting and it has to be a representative slice, right?

00:07:03.240 --> 00:07:08.840
They can't just put the good stuff on top. And so, you know, that's why that window appears.

00:07:08.840 --> 00:07:12.480
It's shocking to me that there's not a window on potato chip bags,

00:07:12.480 --> 00:07:17.180
so that you can see that there's only six potato chips in the bag. But so far we've got the bacon rule.

00:07:17.180 --> 00:07:19.440
We've heard a lot of criticism in the Trump administration

00:07:19.440 --> 00:07:22.580
about this thing called the administrative state,

00:07:22.580 --> 00:07:26.740
this idea that it's not lawmakers making the laws, that our elected representatives

00:07:26.740 --> 00:07:29.400
with a sense of discussion and debate and due process,

00:07:29.400 --> 00:07:33.240
but they're outsourcing things to agencies and federal bureaucracies

00:07:33.240 --> 00:07:37.240
that are actually making the real details of the laws,  and that's ultimately what really matters.

00:07:37.240 --> 00:07:40.400
Is that an implicit critique that you're making in this book?

00:07:40.400 --> 00:07:46.380
Yeah, I mean look article 1 section 1 of the United States Constitution says that Congress has the lawmaking power.

00:07:46.380 --> 00:07:47.880
Period. Full stop.

00:07:47.880 --> 00:07:51.340
It's not theirs to give away. And that's something called the Non-Delegation Doctrine,

00:07:51.340 --> 00:07:55.280
which has sort of been dormant or dead for a number of decades.

00:07:55.280 --> 00:07:58.300
But the truth is, it is Congress's job to make the law.

00:07:58.300 --> 00:08:01.460
They're the lawmaking branch. That's why we call them lawmakers.

00:08:01.460 --> 00:08:05.940
But they have given away, they've ceded that authority to executive branch agencies.

00:08:05.940 --> 00:08:11.280
So now you have the enforcers also doing the regulating, the lawmaking.

00:08:11.280 --> 00:08:14.420
So they're both picking what is a violation of the law,

00:08:14.420 --> 00:08:17.320
and then prosecuting those violations of the law.

00:08:17.320 --> 00:08:21.020
And to be fair like not all the laws in your book are bad necessarily,

00:08:21.020 --> 00:08:24.360
like the law threat against threatening clowns. That's a good law.

00:08:24.360 --> 00:08:25.160
—I'm pro clown.—

00:08:25.160 --> 00:08:29.700
I'm definitely pro clown. Almost anything can be considered a drug paraphernalia.

00:08:29.700 --> 00:08:33.220
Some of those laws are like just overly vague in that way, right?

00:08:33.220 --> 00:08:35.780
Anything can be drug paraphernalia.

00:08:35.780 --> 00:08:41.640
If anybody who had a creative roommate in college knows that anything can be drug paraphernalia.

00:08:41.640 --> 00:08:46.040
But there's a couple of categories that Congress has specifically articulated.

00:08:46.040 --> 00:08:51.680
Like miniature spoons that are less than 1/10 of a cubic centimeter in level capacity,

00:08:51.680 --> 00:08:54.800
and that actually derives from

00:08:54.800 --> 00:09:00.420
some interesting congressional testimony about the McDonald's mix spoon, which was used to stir coffee.

00:09:00.420 --> 00:09:04.040
No I'm very glad they're spending a lot of time on the kind of stuff.

00:09:04.040 --> 00:09:07.380
And it's not just Congress right? Like you mentioned the Powell decision,—

00:09:07.380 --> 00:09:14.140
—the Supreme Court spends a shocking amount of time discussing these sort of absurd laws.

00:09:14.140 --> 00:09:20.120
Why are they doing that? Underneath these absurd laws are there crucial constitutional questions at play?

00:09:20.220 --> 00:09:21.120
Sure there often are.

00:09:21.120 --> 00:09:25.320
I mean one of the main constitutional questions that occurs, not just the federal level,

00:09:25.320 --> 00:09:31.240
but at the state level as well, is this question of void for vagueness, unconstitutional vagueness.

00:09:31.240 --> 00:09:37.640
And so yeah in the Powell decision, the statute basically said "any concealable firearm".

00:09:37.640 --> 00:09:42.440
So that's a crime to mail. You can't mail a concealable firearm. So you and I think,

00:09:42.440 --> 00:09:48.420
well that's clearly a pistol or some small gun that I can put on my body. But in the Powell decision,

00:09:48.420 --> 00:09:54.040
the court was looking at a 22 and a half inch sawed-off shotgun that was sent through the mail and they said,

00:09:54.040 --> 00:09:57.400
well look, if you can dress appropriately, you could probably conceal that.

00:09:57.400 --> 00:10:01.620
And so I don't know what the limits are. There's some big people out there. They could hide a lot of stuff.

00:10:01.620 --> 00:10:05.900
So it's those kind of vague terms that often peak the courts interest.

00:10:05.900 --> 00:10:10.660
Tell us a little bit about yourself. What is your–Why are you an expert in these absurd laws?

00:10:10.660 --> 00:10:14.220
Why should we take your opinion seriously about this crazy stuff?

00:10:14.220 --> 00:10:17.960
I don't know if anybody should take my opinion seriously about this, but I will tell you that

00:10:17.960 --> 00:10:23.940
I'm fun at parties because I spend all my time reading the Code of Federal Regulations in the US Code.

00:10:23.940 --> 00:10:31.020
And so what really it is, I've committed to the grunt work of going through all of these pages

00:10:31.020 --> 00:10:35.260
and reading all these sections and subsections and subparagraphs. So, you know by day

00:10:35.260 --> 00:10:41.420
I'm a white-collar criminal defense lawyer,  that's what they say, but I mean I'll defend anybody for anything.

00:10:41.420 --> 00:10:48.060
But but the truth is that I by night have been spending a lot of time trying to count the number of crimes

00:10:48.060 --> 00:10:51.400
because the Department of Justice said that they couldn't do it.

00:10:51.400 --> 00:10:53.720
They tried in the 80s, and quit.

00:10:53.720 --> 00:10:59.700
And so I said look if I do one a day, I only need like 800 years to finish the job,

00:10:59.700 --> 00:11:03.020
and then I'll be able to say that I counted them all. So that's what I've been doing.

00:11:03.020 --> 00:11:04.700
That's what Twitter is for ultimately.

00:11:04.700 --> 00:11:07.840
Twitter's for that, and destroying other people.

00:11:07.840 --> 00:11:09.640
(Laughs)

00:11:09.640 --> 00:11:13.120
Which continues to be illegal,—Right.—At least for the time being.

00:11:13.120 --> 00:11:19.040
And you do mention in the beginning of the book that this is almost like a legalistic disclaimer.

00:11:19.040 --> 00:11:22.540
Like this book is a book of humor and not serious legal advice.

00:11:22.540 --> 00:11:23.280
—Right.—

00:11:23.280 --> 00:11:26.320
Is there some like—I couldn't figure out why even put that in there.

00:11:26.320 --> 00:11:31.080
Is there some law insisting that someone can sue you if they thought oh, this is legal advice,

00:11:31.080 --> 00:11:32.840
I should commit all these eight hundred crimes?

00:11:32.840 --> 00:11:41.220
The truth is if somebody comes to me and says is it illegal to "x", whatever the blank is filled with,

00:11:41.220 --> 00:11:42.740
The answer is I don't know.

00:11:42.740 --> 00:11:48.080
I can't sign a piece of paper that says, hey it's totally legal to do any particular thing because

00:11:48.080 --> 00:11:53.180
you can't read all the laws. I haven't gotten through all of them yet, even though I've been doing it for five years.

00:11:53.180 --> 00:11:57.360
And so I think what's important is for people who read the book to know it's a work of humor,

00:11:57.360 --> 00:11:59.120
don't do anything in the book.

00:11:59.120 --> 00:12:04.520
Yeah, you're probably not going to get charged for selling, you know, runny ketchup, but you could.

00:12:04.520 --> 00:12:08.100
We'll talk about that later, but yeah.

00:12:08.100 --> 00:12:14.780
It's important that people know that the truth is that the specter of criminal liability hangs over all of us,

00:12:14.780 --> 00:12:15.800
all the time.

00:12:15.800 --> 00:12:18.960
And that's why people find themselves before the courts and even the Supreme Court.

00:12:18.960 --> 00:12:23.100
Are people often even charged with these crimes, or is it extremely rare?

00:12:23.100 --> 00:12:24.780
So it depends, which crimes you're talking about?

00:12:24.780 --> 00:12:25.860
I mean, you know

00:12:25.860 --> 00:12:30.460
We expect prosecutors to exercise something called prosecutorial discretion.

00:12:30.460 --> 00:12:33.920
What that ends up meaning is that they charge mostly gun crimes,

00:12:33.920 --> 00:12:37.060
drug crimes, immigration crimes, and fraud crimes.

00:12:37.060 --> 00:12:39.600
That's the overwhelming majority of it.

00:12:39.600 --> 00:12:44.140
Now say the estimates are one and a half to two percent of the federal docket

00:12:44.140 --> 00:12:48.180
is filled with these administrative crimes, violations of regulations.

00:12:48.180 --> 00:12:50.780
That's still thousands of people each year.

00:12:50.780 --> 00:12:55.640
That's thousands of lives people who have to get a lawyer, go to court, potentially appeal after a conviction.

00:12:55.640 --> 00:12:59.520
Maybe try to go to the Supreme Court to find out if it's unconstitutional.

00:12:59.520 --> 00:13:03.400
So yeah people are charged with them, and I go through a couple of examples in the book

00:13:03.400 --> 00:13:07.020
of people who were charged with things that they never thought would be a federal crime,

00:13:07.020 --> 00:13:12.300
maybe a violation or a small state misdemeanor or something of that effect,

00:13:12.300 --> 00:13:14.320
but not something that would get them a federal criminal record.

00:13:14.320 --> 00:13:18.040
Is it like if a federal prosecutor wants to throw the book at you,

00:13:18.040 --> 00:13:19.780
they will add these charges on?

00:13:19.780 --> 00:13:24.180
If a federal prosecutor wants to throw the book at you, it's my book that they throw,

00:13:24.180 --> 00:13:28.400
but yes, really there's a coercive element to it, right?

00:13:28.400 --> 00:13:33.200
So if we look at these right now, all the discussion is about process crimes right?

00:13:33.200 --> 00:13:36.860
People talk about obstruction of justice, they talk about lying to federal agents.

00:13:36.860 --> 00:13:42.420
The truth is if they can't get you on a substantive crime that everybody agrees is morally reprehensible,

00:13:42.420 --> 00:13:45.160
they can often get you on lying to the feds.

00:13:45.160 --> 00:13:49.620
Because they decide what's a lie, they decide whether they think that you were being truthful or not

00:13:49.620 --> 00:13:53.760
and it's incumbent upon you to defend yourself in in federal court.

00:13:53.760 --> 00:13:58.240
And so yeah, if you go into a meeting with federal prosecutors,

00:13:58.240 --> 00:14:03.520
nobody can tell you that you're not admitting to a federal crime when you describe something that you did,

00:14:03.520 --> 00:14:08.500
because you may not know whether there's a federal regulation or a statue that covers that conduct.

00:14:08.500 --> 00:14:09.300
And that's a problem.

00:14:09.300 --> 00:14:13.720
And you can actually learn a lot about the legal and judicial philosophies of different administrations

00:14:13.720 --> 00:14:14.640
based on this stuff.

00:14:14.640 --> 00:14:20.540
Like for example, the Obama administration was quite fond of the MBTA, the Migratory Bird Acts.

00:14:20.540 --> 00:14:21.040
Right.

00:14:21.100 --> 00:14:21.800
The Migratory Bird—

00:14:21.800 --> 00:14:22.300
Treaty.

00:14:22.360 --> 00:14:23.340
Treaty Act! Thank you.

00:14:23.340 --> 00:14:26.460
And then the Trump administration looked at that same law and said no,

00:14:26.460 --> 00:14:29.180
that's vague and ridiculous and we should strike that down.

00:14:29.180 --> 00:14:32.740
Is that is this a window on two different administrations?

00:14:32.740 --> 00:14:37.460
MBTA situation is one example where, yes the Obama administration said,

00:14:37.460 --> 00:14:39.040
accidental bird deaths,

00:14:39.040 --> 00:14:45.280
so if you get a in a really clean plate glass window and an Egret flies into your window and breaks its neck,

00:14:45.280 --> 00:14:46.900
can you technically be charged?

00:14:46.900 --> 00:14:53.380
Yes, Obama administration said, but we're gonna trust prosecutors not to charge those more ridiculous cases.

00:14:53.380 --> 00:14:58.560
And including, you know, during the Obama administration, a utility was charged

00:14:58.560 --> 00:15:03.040
for a bunch of birds dying in windmills accidentally. Nobody tried to kill those birds.

00:15:03.040 --> 00:15:06.240
But then the Trump administration looked at it and said well, wait a second,

00:15:06.240 --> 00:15:08.680
this is the epitome of vague law.

00:15:08.680 --> 00:15:14.060
If we've just ceded what's a crime and what's not a crime to the executive branch,

00:15:14.060 --> 00:15:16.880
that's sort of over-broad and impermissible.

00:15:16.880 --> 00:15:22.400
So we're issuing new guidance saying that those kind of accidental bird deaths are not crimes.

00:15:22.400 --> 00:15:26.280
Now nothing in the law changed, nothing Congress passed changed.

00:15:26.280 --> 00:15:28.980
You could go look at the text and see the same words.

00:15:28.980 --> 00:15:31.320
But two different administrations have said,

00:15:31.320 --> 00:15:34.900
one is a crime and one's not a crime without a change in the law.

00:15:34.900 --> 00:15:41.560
But what I will tell you is the same Trump administration has used the regulatory power

00:15:41.560 --> 00:15:44.320
to create and change crimes on the books as well.

00:15:44.320 --> 00:15:48.080
We saw the bump stock ban in March.

00:15:48.080 --> 00:15:50.000
Basically with the stroke of a pen,

00:15:50.000 --> 00:15:54.060
essentially doing the exact same thing that the Obama administration was criticized for,

00:15:54.060 --> 00:15:58.420
the Trump administration made a whole new class of previously legal products

00:15:58.420 --> 00:16:02.280
into criminal contraband. Without going through the legislative process.

00:16:02.280 --> 00:16:05.319
So every administration is guilty of it in some respect.

00:16:05.320 --> 00:16:07.160
It's maybe a question of degree.

00:16:07.160 --> 00:16:10.193
It was the Department of Justice that issued the regulation,

00:16:10.193 --> 00:16:13.580
they changed the regulation that interpreted what a machine gun is.

00:16:13.580 --> 00:16:18.340
So essentially by changing the definition of a machine gun, they created a whole new class of crime.

00:16:18.340 --> 00:16:22.820
So on day one you could have legally possessed it, on day two you were a federal criminal.

00:16:22.820 --> 00:16:25.140
Why did you dedicate this book to the US Congress?

00:16:25.140 --> 00:16:26.980
Like any, you know, great comedian,

00:16:26.980 --> 00:16:29.580
I have ghost writers, and Congress is my ghostwriter.

00:16:29.580 --> 00:16:32.300
I couldn't have done any of the book without them.

00:16:32.300 --> 00:16:36.020
And it's because really what they've done is, again,

00:16:36.020 --> 00:16:39.500
I don't think anybody's intentionally created the more ridiculous crimes.

00:16:39.500 --> 00:16:42.420
A lot of them have been created by accident, right.

00:16:42.420 --> 00:16:47.020
The Congress writes a law that says any violation that's a crime,

00:16:47.020 --> 00:16:50.220
and then the regulator's go about their business making regulations.

00:16:50.220 --> 00:16:53.840
And then somebody sits down, like me, and they start tweeting about it,

00:16:53.840 --> 00:17:00.860
and they go technically, you know, you made it a crime to. . .you know, submit a stamp

00:17:00.860 --> 00:17:05.220
in the migratory bird stamp contest and you used computer-generated art.

00:17:05.220 --> 00:17:06.420
You're not allowed to use that.

00:17:06.420 --> 00:17:08.600
You have to paint a duck or something like that.

00:17:08.600 --> 00:17:13.080
But but the truth is they're not looking to create crimes all the time, but they do.

00:17:13.080 --> 00:17:18.500
And they should be—Congress should be more careful about giving away that criminal enforcement power.

00:17:18.500 --> 00:17:22.240
What can be done about this? Is there is a law, another law that needs to be passed?

00:17:22.240 --> 00:17:24.540
How can we slow this sort of thing down?

00:17:24.540 --> 00:17:27.720
Congress can go through and look at the statutes that I cite in the book

00:17:27.720 --> 00:17:33.120
that give away this blanket criminal enforcement power to the agencies,

00:17:33.120 --> 00:17:34.660
and then, it can

00:17:34.660 --> 00:17:37.220
maybe take a look at those statutes because remember,

00:17:37.220 --> 00:17:42.040
the reason that there's 300,000 federal crimes is not because of criminal statues, right?

00:17:42.040 --> 00:17:45.360
There's maybe a couple thousand federal criminal statutes.

00:17:45.360 --> 00:17:50.160
But if they go through those few that give away this blanket authority to regulators to make crimes,

00:17:50.160 --> 00:17:55.640
and change that so that it's required that if something's gonna be criminally punished

00:17:55.640 --> 00:17:58.820
it must specifically say so in the act.

00:17:58.820 --> 00:18:02.280
That would eliminate a huge number of these federal crimes, right?

00:18:02.280 --> 00:18:08.340
In order for a regulator to make it a crime, it has to specifically say so and Congress needs to approve it.

00:18:08.340 --> 00:18:11.020
That would be a good step in the right direction.

00:18:11.020 --> 00:18:14.300
Congress can also look at bad regulations and use this

00:18:14.300 --> 00:18:20.160
little-used act called the Congressional Review Act and they can go through and look at the regulations and say

00:18:20.160 --> 00:18:23.120
we disagree and we're overriding this regulatory action.

00:18:23.120 --> 00:18:25.940
But they don't like to do that because it's not politically popular to do it.

00:18:25.940 --> 00:18:27.780
And you did all the illustrations in this book.

00:18:27.780 --> 00:18:30.060
And the illustrations are really wonderful.

00:18:30.060 --> 00:18:35.900
You have really captured that kind of soul-sucking, bureaucratic diagram

00:18:35.900 --> 00:18:39.280
that tells people, tells people what to do and how to do it, right?

00:18:39.280 --> 00:18:40.160
Right, yeah.

00:18:40.220 --> 00:18:41.620
Is that the look you were going for?

00:18:41.620 --> 00:18:42.680
Yeah, exactly.

00:18:42.680 --> 00:18:45.460
I mean and in fact if you walk through the Code of Federal Regulations,

00:18:45.460 --> 00:18:48.760
you'll even see some similar type illustrations.

00:18:48.760 --> 00:18:56.180
It's just this Government, you know, no joy, expressionless, sometimes faceless figure,

00:18:56.180 --> 00:18:57.460
doing some prohibited act.

00:18:57.460 --> 00:18:59.040
Yeah no color either. That's very important.

00:18:59.040 --> 00:19:00.220
Yeah that's very important.

00:19:00.220 --> 00:19:04.360
Yeah color might suggest hope or some sort of positivity.

00:19:04.360 --> 00:19:06.920
And I wanted to crush that, stamp that out of the reader.

00:19:06.920 --> 00:19:08.300
Yeah, that made me feel good.

00:19:08.300 --> 00:19:09.180
Yeah.

00:19:09.180 --> 00:19:10.360
Mike Chase, thank you very much.

00:19:10.360 --> 00:19:14.540
The book is "How to Be a Federal Criminal" and it's out in bookstores?

00:19:14.540 --> 00:19:15.200
June Fourth.

00:19:15.200 --> 00:19:17.260
Very good. I'm Todd Krainin for Reason.

